WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:05.970
Movies take a staggering amount of money to
make, and nowadays, any budget below $50 million

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:08.060
is considered mid-to-low range.

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:12.360
That level of spending usually works out in
the end, since movies also make a staggering

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:16.400
amount of money, but not all producers are
lucky enough to earn back their investment.

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:20.840
In the case of these films, the films did
so poorly at the box office that they're considered

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:22.780
cautionary tales in Tinseltown.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.420
The 13th Warrior

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:30.020
Adapted from a Michael Crichton novel, The
13th Warrior is loosely based upon the myth

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:30.660
of Beowulf.

00:00:31.029 --> 00:00:35.880
While the film earned some recognition for
its expensive set pieces, those costly moments

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:41.180
ended up ballooning the film's budget to $160
million, while the box office returns were

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:46.720
only $61.7 million, leaving the film with
a deficit of almost $100 million.

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:51.080
The movie's failure to find an audience is
a bit of a puzzler, since the source material

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:55.900
was by a proven bestselling writer and it
was directed by Die Hard's John McTiernan.

00:00:56.110 --> 00:01:00.460
But no matter the reason, the end results
were so embarrassing that co-star Omar Sharif

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:03.280
ended up briefly retiring from acting in disgust.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:05.640
The Adventures of Pluto Nash

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:11.240
Eddie Murphy used to be a reliable hit-maker
with successes in everything from action adventure

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:16.020
films to zany comedies to movies that required
him to suit up for almost every character.

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:20.960
He even had some successes on the family film
scene with Dr. Doolittle and Shrek confirming

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:23.420
that kiddos liked to laugh at him just as
much as adults.

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:28.060
But his star power burnt out somewhere between
those films and The Adventures of Pluto Nash,

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:32.940
because the film earned back just $7.1 million
of its $100 million budget.

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:35.600
It's not hard to see why the film fared so
poorly.

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:40.520
With mediocre special effects, a confusing
and unfunny script, and a nonsensical future

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:44.800
setting, even Eddie Murphy's big name couldn't
save the film from flopping hard.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:47.100
Stealth

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:52.000
Rob Cohen was behind the lens for a number
of hits, including The Fast and the Furious

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:53.180
and xXx.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:58.700
But his box office streak took a serious nosedive
when his aviation actioner Stealth crashed

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:00.240
and burned at the box office.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:05.050
The film followed a crew of Air Force pilots
trying to control an Artificial Intelligence

00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:05.550
jet.

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:09.420
But instead of being a compelling adventure
movie, it was more like a science-fiction

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:10.680
parody of Top Gun.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:17.350
It grossed just $76 million against a $135
million budget and made good on its title

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:20.400
by sneaking away from the theaters largely
unnoticed.

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:21.680
Sahara

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:26.460
Based on a massively successful series of
books about a rugged adventurer with a quick

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:30.660
wit and a way with women, Sahara looked like
it might be able to recapture the magic of

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:31.700
Indiana Jones.

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:36.400
Unfortunately for everyone involved, the built-in
audience for the books didn't translate to

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:42.400
a box office success and Sahara grossed a
modest $119.3 million that wasn't enough to

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:47.100
cover its $160 million budget and $81 million
in distribution costs.

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:51.040
Part of it could have just been poor timing,
since the movie starred Matthew McConaughey

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:54.480
before his star power returned in full force
with the McConaissance.

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:57.340
"It'd be a lot cooler if you did."

00:02:57.340 --> 00:03:00.780
But either way, the studio was parched after
this movie's returns.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:02.880
Mars Needs Moms

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:09.200
Disney's experiment with motion-capture performance,
co-produced by legendary director Robert Zemeckis,

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:10.660
seemed like a good idea on paper.

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:15.660
And Mars Needs Moms wasn't conceptually off
the mark, either, as it followed a nine-year-old

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:20.470
trying to save his mom from aliens who want
to harness her powers to juice up their "nannybots,"

00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:22.820
which is the stuff of fairly standard family
fare.

00:03:22.910 --> 00:03:27.000
But while the film's effects were cutting
edge, they gave an almost uncomfortable eeriness

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.060
to every character's facial expressions.

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:33.280
And rather than an exciting adventure or a
surreal nightmare of a film, the movie ended

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:34.300
up being a snooze.

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:39.300
Audiences on Earth didn't have much interest
in it, handing over just $39 million in ticket

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:42.440
fare against a whopping $150 million budget.

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:46.460
The Mouse House might not usually have trouble
wrangling in the kiddos to see their animated

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:49.920
features, but this movie marked a major write-down
for the studio.

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:52.480
Speaking of Disney flops in outer space ...

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:54.060
John Carter

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:59.060
Based on the seminal works of Edgar Rice Burroughs,
which influenced everything from Star Wars

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:03.140
to parts of the DC Universe, John Carter seemed
like a sure thing on paper.

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:06.680
In practice, though, the film fet ironically
derivative and trite.

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:12.400
John Carter managed to make $73 million domestically,
with worldwide sales barely pushing the film

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:15.020
past its $250 million budget.

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:20.140
Considering it took around $100 million more
to market the movie, Disney took a hefty loss

00:04:20.150 --> 00:04:22.280
on the would-be tentpole project.

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:26.871
The title hero of the film might be less affected
by gravity on Mars, but here on Earth, he

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:28.380
was brought down hard.

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:29.780
R.I.P.D.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:35.060
The title of this film is an acronym for the
Rest In Peace Department, a special police

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:39.060
force that takes care of renegade spirits
before they can affect the human world.

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:43.680
It's basically Men in Black with a supernatural
twist, and the casting of Ryan Reynolds as

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:47.820
the upstart partner to Jeff Bridges' grumpy
mentor seemed like a no-brainer.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:53.550
Unfortunately, the movie was pronounced D.O.A.,
barely making back $78 million of its $130

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:54.520
million budget.

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:59.260
While the failure of certain films can sometimes
be attributed to poor marketing, studio interference,

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:04.150
or even rival movie releases that are oddly
similar, the blame for R.I.P.D. rests pretty

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:07.440
entirely on the film itself—it's relentlessly
unfunny.

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:11.200
Bridges and Reynolds seem like they're so
bored with the script that they might as well

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:12.500
be on the other side.

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:15.280
But, hey, maybe they were just method acting
the whole time.

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:21.100
"tough day."

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:23.240
The Lone Ranger

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:27.880
The character The Lone Ranger is a classic
part of American pop culture.

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:33.320
Unfortunately for the makers of the 2013 reboot
of the hit TV and radio series, however, the

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:35.670
film was plagued with problems from start
to finish.

00:05:35.670 --> 00:05:40.910
For starters, the film drew ire over casting
Johnny Depp as the Native American sidekick

00:05:40.910 --> 00:05:45.460
Tonto, which prompted massive criticism over
its decision to whitewash the character.

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:51.600
Then, there was the matter of its bloated
budget, which was $215 million before marketing

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:52.100
costs.

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:56.120
And to top it all off, critics laughed at
the film for all the wrong reasons.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:03.360
"Justice is what I seek kemosabe"
Maybe just... pick a different hat next time.

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:04.780
Thanks for watching!

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:07.560
